--------sizeof(pointer) in C (Simple Explanation)

One of the most asked MCQ topics in C interviews.

What does sizeof(pointer) return?

sizeof(pointer) returns the size of the pointer variable itself, NOT the size of the data it points to.

Example 1
#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a = 10;
    int *ptr = &a;

    printf("%zu", sizeof(ptr));
}
Output (Most 64-bit systems)
8

Why?

Because ptr stores an address, and on a 64-bit system, an address is 8 bytes.

Example 2
char ch = 'A';
char *ptr = &ch;

printf("%zu", sizeof(ptr));

Output (64-bit):

8

Even though char is only 1 byte, the pointer still occupies 8 bytes.

Example 3
double d = 10.5;
double *ptr = &d;

printf("%zu", sizeof(ptr));

Output (64-bit):

8

Again, the pointer size is unchanged.

Why?

A pointer stores only a memory address.

An address has a fixed size on a given system.

It doesn't matter whether the pointer points to:

char
int
float
double
struct

The pointer size remains the same on that architecture.

32-bit vs 64-bit Systems
System	Pointer Size
32-bit	4 Bytes
64-bit	8 Bytes
